WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.810
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.520
I'm going to try to do this without a script. So the other day I was talking to a YouTube friend of mine,

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.060
who also has OCD, and they were like, "How do you seem so put together?"

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:13.240
Which is weird because, at the exact same moment, I was thinking the exact same thought about them.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.500
Anyway, the answer, of course, for both of us, is:

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.500
Editing

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:17.126
Like you hear me say:

00:00:17.126 --> 00:00:20.080
"Good morning. Hank. It's Tuesday. I'm going to try to do this without a script."

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.520
But like what you don't hear is this:

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:27.820
"Good morning. Good morning. Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday. So the oth--g-good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday, so the other day-

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:33.100
G-good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday. So the other day I was talking to a YouTube friend of-- Good morning. Good morning. (exasperated sigh)

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:38.260
Good morning. Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday. So the other day I was talking to a YouTube friend of-- Good morning. Good morning.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.731
That goes on for about 2 minutes and 40 seconds.

00:00:40.731 --> 00:00:45.600
So like this is real, I'm in my real basement right now, and I'm going to really edit this video and upload it.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.360
But it's also inevitably a construction, like I'm picking this frame.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.740
By the way, why are there so many new boxes?

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:55.720
And if we move the frame just a little bit, you would see that, like, most of the basement is toys.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.280
It's almost impossible to remember on like a second-by-second basis that when you watch a YouTube video

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.400
or when you look at people's social media feeds,

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.400
you're seeing a curated version of those people.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.300
We talk a lot in the YouTube age about authenticity and over the last decade, I have tried to be authentic.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.280
But in telling the truth, you choose among many true stories which true story to tell, right?

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.260
Like, let me tell you two stories, both of which are true.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:23.420
Last week, I went to VidCon Europe in Amsterdam. And it was awesome and amazing and I had such a good time --true.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:29.320
Also true: I was infuriated with myself that I wasn't able to do a signing because of these problems I have in my brain.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:34.400
In "On Photography", Susan Sontag writes about the farm security administration's photo project in the 1930s.

00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:38.620
She says the photographers would take dozens of frontal pictures of one of their sharecropper subjects,

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:42.000
until satisfied that they had gotten just the right look on film --

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:50.860
"the precise expression on the subject's face that supported their own notions about poverty, light, dignity, texture, exploitation, and geometry".

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:56.780
Making videos, even ones that don't feature your face, is often a kind of self portraiture, as is your Twitter or your Facebook.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.500
It's you, but it's also framed.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.320
And images are just so incredibly compelling,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:07.300
like we've all had the experience of reading a novel and imagining the characters in our own minds and then seeing a movie adaptation

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:10.760
and having the images we imagined to be replaced by the actors.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:14.100
But an image is just that: it is not the thing itself.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.780
This is not a pipe, and this is my life. But it's not all of my life.

00:02:17.789 --> 00:02:22.240
And if I can remember that about myself, maybe I can start to understand it about other people.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.910
By the way, over at the 100days channel,

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:29.180
I made a video talking about my OCD in much more detail, if that's something that would interest you. Link in the dooblydoo below.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:34.140
And remember, the next time you see an Instagram photo that you're super jealous of, somebody framed that shot.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.860
As indeed, we are all framing our lives.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.640
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

